Server code has historically faced a forced choice:

**Option A — Thread-per-request with platform threads**:
- Easy programming model: write blocking code, one thread handles one request start to finish.
- Doesn't scale: platform threads cost ~1 MB stack + kernel bookkeeping; you can create ~few thousand before running out of memory or hitting OS limits.
- Under load, a Tomcat with 200 worker threads rejects requests when all are blocked on slow DB calls, even though CPUs are idle.

**Option B — Async/reactive with a small thread pool**:
- Scales: a handful of threads multiplex millions of connections via non-blocking I/O (Netty, Reactor, WebFlux).
- Painful programming: callbacks / `CompletableFuture` chains / `Mono`/`Flux`. Stack traces unreadable. ThreadLocal broken. Debugging nightmare.
- The "colored function" problem: everything must be async all the way down.

**Virtual threads give you the best of both**: blocking code (Option A) with the scaling of async (Option B). The JVM does the multiplexing automatically.

```java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            var response = httpClient.send(request, BodyHandlers.ofString());
            process(response.body());
        });
    }
}
```

A million blocking HTTP calls, concurrently, on a laptop. No callbacks. No reactive glue. Just code that reads top-to-bottom.

Three ways to start one:

**1. `Thread.ofVirtual()` builder** — direct creation:
```java
Thread vt = Thread.ofVirtual()
    .name("worker-", 0) // auto-increment counter
    .start(() -> handleRequest());
vt.join();
```

**2. `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()`** — the idiomatic executor form:
```java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    List<Future<String>> results = urls.stream()
        .map(url -> executor.submit(() -> fetch(url)))
        .toList();
    for (Future<String> f : results) {
        System.out.println(f.get());
    }
} // close() waits for all tasks
```

This executor **creates a new virtual thread per task** (no pooling needed — they're cheap enough). No configurable pool size, because there's no reason to limit them.

**3. `Thread.startVirtualThread(runnable)`** — one-liner:
```java
Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> doWork());
```

**Detecting virtual**: `Thread.currentThread().isVirtual()` returns true.

**Not a replacement for `ExecutorService` for bounded work** — if you want a semaphore-style rate limit, use a `Semaphore` or a traditional bounded `ThreadPoolExecutor`. Virtual-thread-per-task executors are unbounded by design.

The biggest production concern with virtual threads is **carrier pinning**. When a virtual thread enters a `synchronized` block and then blocks on I/O, the JVM cannot unmount it — the carrier thread is **pinned** and unavailable for other virtual threads.

This effectively turns the carrier pool into a traditional thread pool with the same concurrency limit. If all carriers are pinned, the system has no threads available for new work — worse than a standard thread pool because the expectation was unlimited concurrency.

```java
// PROBLEMATIC: synchronized pins the carrier
synchronized (lock) {
    result = httpClient.send(request); // I/O blocks while pinned
}

// BETTER: ReentrantLock does not pin
lock.lock();
try {
    result = httpClient.send(request); // I/O unmounts the virtual thread
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
```

The migration path: replace `synchronized` blocks that contain I/O with `ReentrantLock`. Synchronized blocks with pure computation (no blocking) are fine — they complete quickly and pinning is brief.

Creating virtual threads is straightforward: `Thread.startVirtualThread(runnable)`, `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()`, or `Thread.ofVirtual().start(runnable)`. Do **not** pool virtual threads — pooling defeats the purpose. Create one per task, let the JVM manage carrier threads.

Use `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=short` to log pinning events during testing. This identifies synchronized blocks that need migration before production deployment.

```java
public class UserCache {
    private final Map<String, User> cache = new HashMap<>();
    private final UserRepo repo;

    // PINS on virtual threads
    public synchronized User getOrLoad(String id) {
        User u = cache.get(id);
        if (u == null) {
            u = repo.findById(id); // blocking JDBC call
            // Virtual thread is PINNED here because we're in synchronized
            // Carrier is held for the entire DB call duration
            cache.put(id, u);
        }
        return u;
    }
}
```

With a platform thread pool of 200, this was fine — worst case is 200 threads sequentially waiting on the lock.

With virtual threads, suppose the DB is slow (200 ms per call). If 10 requests hit a cache miss simultaneously:
- 10 virtual threads enter the synchronized block and serialize on the monitor.
- While each is inside, its carrier is pinned during the 200 ms JDBC call.
- If you only have 8 carriers (8 CPUs), **every carrier is busy waiting on DB**.
- Other virtual threads trying to run anything else can't — no free carriers.
- The server appears frozen. CPU at 0%. `jstack` shows all carriers waiting on JDBC.

This is **much worse than the platform-thread version** — you've turned a bounded-concurrency problem into a global carrier starvation.

Before migrating a significant codebase to virtual threads, audit two things:

**1. `synchronized` blocks around I/O**:
```java
synchronized void updateUserCache(String userId) {
    User u = db.findById(userId); // blocking I/O INSIDE synchronized
    cache.put(userId, u);
}
```
On a virtual thread, this **pins** the carrier for the entire DB call. A handful of these can starve all your carriers. Enable JDK Flight Recorder pinning events to find them:
```
-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+RecordJvmLoomPinning
```
Fix by migrating to `ReentrantLock`:
```java
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
void updateUserCache(String userId) {
    lock.lock();
    try {
        User u = db.findById(userId); // unmounts fine
        cache.put(userId, u);
    } finally { lock.unlock(); }
}
```

**2. `ThreadLocal` heavy state**:
Each virtual thread carries its own ThreadLocals. A million virtual threads × 1 KB of ThreadLocal = 1 GB of heap overhead. Audit:
- Large cached objects in ThreadLocal (per-thread buffer pools, SimpleDateFormat) — these are anti-patterns even without Loom.
- Logging MDC is fine (small).
- For immutable per-scope values, migrate to `ScopedValue` (Java 21 preview, stable in later releases) which is efficient at scale.

With platform threads, your 200-thread pool implicitly rate-limited concurrent DB connections to ~200. Virtual threads remove that limit — a million concurrent requests can all try to query the DB at once, and your **20-connection HikariCP pool will be the new bottleneck**, with requests queuing and timing out.

**Mitigation**: add explicit flow control.

```java
Semaphore dbPermits = new Semaphore(50); // max 50 concurrent DB calls

User fetchUser(String id) {
    dbPermits.acquireUninterruptibly();
    try {
        return userRepo.findById(id);
    } finally { dbPermits.release(); }
}
```

Or use a library like **Resilience4j Bulkhead**. Ensure connection pools (HikariCP, RestTemplate's client connection manager) are sized appropriately.

The mindset shift: **thread count no longer limits concurrency, so you must add explicit limits wherever downstream resources are bounded** — DB connections, remote API quotas, memory for request buffering.

```java
public class UserCache {
    private final Map<String, User> cache = new HashMap<>();
    private final UserRepo repo;

    // PINS on virtual threads
    public synchronized User getOrLoad(String id) {
        User u = cache.get(id);
        if (u == null) {
            u = repo.findById(id); // blocking JDBC call
            // Virtual thread is PINNED here because we're in synchronized
            // Carrier is held for the entire DB call duration
            cache.put(id, u);
        }
        return u;
    }
}
```

With a platform thread pool of 200, this was fine — worst case is 200 threads sequentially waiting on the lock.

With virtual threads, suppose the DB is slow (200 ms per call). If 10 requests hit a cache miss simultaneously:
- 10 virtual threads enter the synchronized block and serialize on the monitor.
- While each is inside, its carrier is pinned during the 200 ms JDBC call.
- If you only have 8 carriers (8 CPUs), **every carrier is busy waiting on DB**.
- Other virtual threads trying to run anything else can't — no free carriers.
- The server appears frozen. CPU at 0%. `jstack` shows all carriers waiting on JDBC.

This is **much worse than the platform-thread version** — you've turned a bounded-concurrency problem into a global carrier starvation.

```java
public class UserCache {
    private final Map<String, User> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final UserRepo repo;

    public User getOrLoad(String id) {
        User u = cache.get(id);
        if (u != null) return u;

        lock.lock();
        try {
            // Double-check after acquiring lock
            u = cache.get(id);
            if (u == null) {
                u = repo.findById(id); // unmounts correctly now
                cache.put(id, u);
            }
            return u;
        } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }
}
```

Why this works:
- `ReentrantLock` uses `LockSupport.park()` internally, which is **Loom-aware** — it triggers the continuation yield.
- The virtual thread can unmount while holding the lock, freeing the carrier.
- Other virtual threads can run on that carrier in the meantime.
- When JDBC completes, the virtual thread is rescheduled and releases the lock.

**Better still**: use `ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent` with a `Supplier` — but be careful, it holds a bucket lock during the lambda, which on some implementations can also pin. Measure. For typical sizes, a top-level `ReentrantLock` or per-key striping is safer.
